   "_comment_value_drop_t1_hall_4": "T1-HALL-4, 2026-08-30, JUDGE-6 defect 1. The works' own exterior half of the value drop described in building_prefabs.json's `_why_value_drop_t1_hall_4` -- read that first, it carries the measurement and the reasoning. `stone_light` #9c9083 -> #5f5e58 and `stone_skirt` #8f8172 -> #575147 are the SAME factor the kit's LightRock/DarkRock take, applied here so the ladder HALL_DESIGN sec5 authored and `_comment_stone_light`/`_comment_stone_skirt` below both describe survives the drop intact: skirt < works walls < DarkRock < LightRock is unchanged in order, and the works-to-LightRock step stays 1.36x where it was 1.35x. What moves is the family's absolute value, because JUDGE-6 measured the fortress at five luminance points off its own background and the reference at a 32-point separation with the fortress dark. `stone` (#6a6157) is DELIBERATELY NOT DROPPED and that is not an oversight. `_wall_material` hands `stone_light` to outer walls and `stone` to inner ones, so `stone` is an interior tone, and `_ceiling_colour`'s own note records what happened the last time this building's interiors lost value: the warden arena went to 96.7% of pixels below luminance 40 and a blind critic's first finding was the value crush. The exterior is now slightly darker than the interior, which is both the intended read (a weathered outside, lit rooms within) and the only way to take the silhouette without paying for it in the five rooms the player fights in. ROUND 2, same lane, after rendering: #5f5e58 -> #66655e, giving back about 8% of the drop on the WORKS WALLS ONLY. The kit's LightRock/DarkRock keep their full drop, so the fortress mass that carries the silhouette at range is unchanged. The reason is JUDGE-6 defect 5: the west keep elevation is works wall, it is the frame's shaded face, and the full drop took it to mean L 19.8 with a high-pass texture sd of 2.67 against 28.8/4.10 on the judged frames -- i.e. the value drop bought the silhouette partly at the cost of the defect immediately below it on the same list. This is the smaller half of the correction (see art.json's ambient_sky_contribution round-2 note for the larger half); it is deliberately small so the ladder this file's round-1 note describes still holds -- works-to-LightRock is 1.26x where round 1 put it at 1.36x and the shipped build had 1.35x, so the two stones still read as one fortress.",
   "_comment_stone_light": "T1-HALL-BUILD (2026-08-30), HALL_DESIGN_2026-08-30.md §5's palette-proof step: `stone_light` (the works' own exterior wall / exterior-face-skin tone, `stronghold.gd::_stone_light`/`_exterior_face_material`) moves from the code default #786d5e to the design's #9c9083, so the works' true exterior wears the SAME value-ladder step as the castle kit's retuned walls -- 'one stone, one scale, one value ladder, both kits'. `stone`/`stone_dark`/`floor_colour` are interior tones this pass does not touch; nothing else in `site` changed.",
   "_comment_stone_skirt": "T1-HALL-REBUILD (2026-08-30), HALL_DESIGN_2026-08-30.md §5's foundation tier -- the darkest step on the ladder (skirt < works walls < DarkRock < LightRock < coping), at the coarser 0.22 tile so the foundation reads as BIGGER blocks than the wall above it. It is worn by a facing skin (`stronghold.gd::_build_skirt_facing`), not by the skirt slab itself: each chamber's floor slab IS its skirt (one box 18m deep whose top face is the room's walkable floor), so retinting the slab would repaint five interiors. Before this the building's whole visible plinth rendered as yard cobble seen edge-on, at a smaller stone scale than the wall standing on it -- the exact scale collision §5 resolves with a stated ladder.",
